WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
Turn a hearing aid up as far as

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.240
it'll go, and some people still can't make

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.720
out a single word.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.520
Volume isn't their problem.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.640
Somewhere between the air and the brain, one

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.440
part has stopped working, and a device only

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.520
helps if it gets the signal past that

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:16.200
part.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.400
In 1978, a team in Melbourne found a

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.120
way past one of those breaks.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.720
So how can technology assist hearing loss?

00:00:25.934 --> 00:00:27.573
Let's follow the sound.

00:00:27.854 --> 00:00:30.253
The outer ear funnels sound waves down the

00:00:30.334 --> 00:00:33.133
ear canal to the eardrum, a thin membrane

00:00:33.214 --> 00:00:34.333
that vibrates with them.

00:00:34.814 --> 00:00:37.533
Behind it, in the middle ear, three tiny

00:00:37.614 --> 00:00:41.133
bones called the ossicles pass that vibration along:

00:00:41.214 --> 00:00:43.773
malleus, incus, then stapes.

00:00:44.254 --> 00:00:46.653
The stapes pushes on a small window into

00:00:46.734 --> 00:00:47.533
the inner ear.

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:50.573
That window is much smaller than the eardrum,

00:00:50.654 --> 00:00:53.293
so the pressure is concentrated, and that's what

00:00:53.374 --> 00:00:54.893
moves the fluid inside.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.420
In the inner ear, the cochlea is a

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.420
coiled, fluid-filled tube.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.700
As the stapes pushes, waves travel through the

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.900
fluid and make a membrane inside vibrate.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:09.220
High sounds move it most near the start

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.260
of the coil, and low sounds further along.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.140
Sitting on that membrane are hair cells, the

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:16.180
receptors here.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.860
When it moves, their tiny hairs bend, ion

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.580
channels open, and the cell triggers signals in

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.620
the auditory nerve, which carries them to the

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:25.140
brain.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.180
So the cochlea is where vibration becomes a

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:29.140
nerve signal.

00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:32.926
That gives two main kinds of hearing loss.

00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:35.966
In conductive loss, sound can't get through the

00:01:36.127 --> 00:01:37.486
outer or middle ear.

00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:40.366
The canal might be blocked, fluid might fill

00:01:40.407 --> 00:01:43.086
the middle ear after an infection, the eardrum

00:01:43.207 --> 00:01:45.406
might be torn, or the stapes might be

00:01:45.487 --> 00:01:46.086
stuck.

00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:47.886
The cochlea still works.

00:01:48.687 --> 00:01:51.646
In sensorineural loss, the hair cells or the

00:01:51.727 --> 00:01:55.406
auditory nerve are damaged, by loud noise, ageing,

00:01:55.487 --> 00:01:57.326
genes or some infections.

00:01:57.967 --> 00:02:00.446
Human hair cells don't grow back, so this

00:02:00.527 --> 00:02:02.366
loss is usually permanent.

00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:05.646
Now match a device to the break.

00:02:05.887 --> 00:02:08.366
A hearing aid sits behind or in the

00:02:08.447 --> 00:02:09.006
ear.

00:02:09.247 --> 00:02:12.526
A microphone picks up sound, an amplifier makes

00:02:12.607 --> 00:02:15.006
it louder, mostly at the pitches the person

00:02:15.087 --> 00:02:17.966
hears worst, and a tiny speaker sends it

00:02:18.047 --> 00:02:19.406
down the canal.

00:02:19.487 --> 00:02:22.606
That louder sound still travels the normal path,

00:02:22.687 --> 00:02:24.526
so it needs some hair cells that still

00:02:24.607 --> 00:02:25.406
work.

00:02:25.487 --> 00:02:28.646
It can help when enough usable hearing remains.

00:02:28.687 --> 00:02:31.086
But if most hair cells are gone, louder

00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:33.326
sound has nothing left to stimulate.

00:02:34.534 --> 00:02:37.333
A cochlear implant skips the hair cells.

00:02:37.574 --> 00:02:40.213
A processor behind the ear turns sound into

00:02:40.294 --> 00:02:42.133
a coded electrical signal.

00:02:42.374 --> 00:02:44.613
A coil sends it through the skin, by

00:02:44.694 --> 00:02:47.573
radio, to a receiver under the skin.

00:02:47.614 --> 00:02:49.893
The receiver sends pulses down a thin array

00:02:49.934 --> 00:02:52.693
of electrodes threaded along the cochlea.

00:02:52.774 --> 00:02:55.333
Each electrode stimulates the nerve fibres at its

00:02:55.574 --> 00:02:58.053
position, so electrodes near the start of the

00:02:58.134 --> 00:03:00.133
coil signal high pitches.

00:03:00.214 --> 00:03:02.853
The hair cells are bypassed, but the auditory

00:03:02.934 --> 00:03:04.373
nerve must still work.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.360
This is an Australian story.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.880
In 1978, Graeme Clark and his team at

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.480
the University of Melbourne implanted their multi-channel cochlear

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.600
implant in their first patient, and that work

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.760
grew into implants now used around the world.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.760
But an implant doesn't restore normal hearing.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.680
About twelve to twenty-two electrical channels replace the

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.680
richer coding of about three and a half

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.800
thousand inner hair cells, so the sound is

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:33.520
coarser.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.400
The brain learns to use it over months,

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.040
and music is often harder than speech.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.920
It also needs surgery, which can damage hearing

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.040
left in that ear.

00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:46.666
The third device uses a route you already

00:03:46.827 --> 00:03:47.706
use.

00:03:47.787 --> 00:03:49.786
You hear your own voice partly through the

00:03:49.907 --> 00:03:51.386
bones of your skull.

00:03:51.467 --> 00:03:54.026
A bone conduction implant works that way.

00:03:54.107 --> 00:03:56.666
A small titanium screw is fixed into the

00:03:56.747 --> 00:03:59.226
skull bone behind the ear, and the bone

00:03:59.267 --> 00:04:00.506
grows onto it.

00:04:00.587 --> 00:04:03.226
A sound processor attached to it turns sound

00:04:03.307 --> 00:04:05.946
into vibration, which travels through the skull to

00:04:06.027 --> 00:04:07.146
the cochlea.

00:04:07.227 --> 00:04:09.306
It skips the outer and middle ear, so

00:04:09.387 --> 00:04:11.866
it suits conductive or mixed loss.

00:04:11.947 --> 00:04:14.146
It can also help deafness in one ear,

00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:17.146
by sending sound across to the working cochlea.

00:04:17.387 --> 00:04:19.386
But it needs a working cochlea.

00:04:20.601 --> 00:04:24.000
So the three devices aren't ranked, they're matched.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.160
A hearing aid makes sound louder, so enough

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.360
of the pathway must still work.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.040
A bone conduction implant skips the outer and

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.760
middle ear, so the cochlea must work.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.800
A cochlear implant skips the hair cells, so

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.400
the nerve must work.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:42.600
For a baby born deaf, implanting early gives

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.480
spoken language the best start, but the child

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:46.760
can't consent.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.600
Many Deaf people use Auslan, Australian Sign Language,

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.400
and see deafness as a culture and community,

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:55.160
so families weigh both.

00:04:56.367 --> 00:04:58.046
Two exam questions.

00:04:58.367 --> 00:04:59.046
First.

00:04:59.327 --> 00:05:03.566
A forty-five-year-old has otosclerosis: bone growth has fixed

00:05:03.647 --> 00:05:06.046
the stapes, so it can't vibrate.

00:05:06.367 --> 00:05:09.086
Tests show the cochlea and auditory nerve work

00:05:09.167 --> 00:05:10.206
normally.

00:05:10.367 --> 00:05:12.766
Identify the type of hearing loss.

00:05:12.927 --> 00:05:15.646
Then explain why a bone conduction implant could

00:05:15.727 --> 00:05:18.286
help, and why a cochlear implant would not

00:05:18.367 --> 00:05:19.566
be suitable.

00:05:19.727 --> 00:05:20.926
Pause here to answer.

00:05:31.334 --> 00:05:35.253
It's conductive loss: the stuck stapes stops vibration

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:38.053
reaching the cochlea, which still works, and so

00:05:38.134 --> 00:05:38.933
does the nerve.

00:05:39.574 --> 00:05:42.293
A bone conduction implant sends vibration through the

00:05:42.374 --> 00:05:45.653
skull straight to that cochlea, skipping the stapes.

00:05:46.294 --> 00:05:49.413
A cochlear implant would bypass healthy hair cells,

00:05:49.494 --> 00:05:51.893
and surgery could damage their natural hearing.

00:05:52.534 --> 00:05:55.173
The classic mistake is choosing the cochlear implant

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:56.933
because it's the most advanced.

00:05:58.134 --> 00:05:58.693
Second.

00:05:59.174 --> 00:06:02.373
A sixty-year-old worked for decades in loud factories

00:06:02.694 --> 00:06:05.733
and now has profound sensorineural loss in both

00:06:05.814 --> 00:06:06.853
ears.

00:06:07.014 --> 00:06:09.973
Powerful hearing aids gave almost no benefit, and

00:06:10.054 --> 00:06:13.013
tests show both auditory nerves work.

00:06:13.294 --> 00:06:15.973
Evaluate a cochlear implant for this person.

00:06:16.134 --> 00:06:17.413
Pause here to answer.

00:06:27.834 --> 00:06:30.553
Noise has destroyed most of the hair cells,

00:06:30.634 --> 00:06:33.473
so louder sound has little left to stimulate.

00:06:33.754 --> 00:06:36.793
An implant bypasses them and stimulates the working

00:06:36.874 --> 00:06:40.073
nerves directly, giving access to sound.

00:06:40.114 --> 00:06:43.353
But it won't be normal hearing: few electrodes,

00:06:43.514 --> 00:06:46.233
months of learning, and surgery that risks any

00:06:46.314 --> 00:06:47.593
hearing left.

00:06:47.674 --> 00:06:50.393
On balance, it's a strong choice here.

00:06:50.474 --> 00:06:52.713
The classic mistake is writing that the implant

00:06:52.794 --> 00:06:54.553
restores normal hearing.

00:06:55.767 --> 00:06:58.806
So for any hearing technology question, follow the

00:06:58.967 --> 00:07:00.006
sound to the break.

00:07:00.487 --> 00:07:03.206
Ask what still works, then choose the device

00:07:03.287 --> 00:07:05.606
that delivers the signal just past the broken

00:07:05.687 --> 00:07:06.446
part.

00:07:06.887 --> 00:07:09.526
Next, the same idea for the eye: how

00:07:09.607 --> 00:07:12.086
technology can assist visual disorders.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:14.740
Here are the key notes.

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:16.900
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:17.780
to copy them.
